     mmaakkee reads a set of dependency rules and shell commands and
     uses them to decide which _t_a_r_g_e_t files are out of date with
     respect to the files they depend on, and to bring them up to
     date by running the associated commands.

     Arguments of the form _m_a_c_r_o= appearing before the first _t_a_r-
     _g_e_t define _m_a_c_r_o for the duration of the run, overriding any
     definition of the same name inside the makefile.

     Unless -rr is given, mmaakkee first reads /uussrr/lliibb/mmaakkeemmaaccrrooss to
     establish the built-in macros and suffix rules, then reads
     the makefile itself, then reads /uussrr/lliibb/mmaakkeeaaccttiioonnss to es-
     tablish the built-in target actions.  The makefile is read
     from each -ff _f_i_l_e given, in order; if no -ff is given, mmaakkee
     looks first for mmaakkeeffiillee and then for MMaakkeeffiillee in the cur-
     rent directory.

     If the makefile defines the special target .IIGGNNOORREE or
     .SSIILLEENNTT, mmaakkee behaves as though -ii or -ss respectively had
     also been given.  If it defines .DDEEFFAAUULLTT, the commands at-
     tached to that target are used to make any target for which
     no other rule applies.

     After the makefile is read, mmaakkee brings each named _t_a_r_g_e_t up
     to date, in the order given; with no _t_a_r_g_e_t argument, it
     brings up to date the first target defined in the makefile.

