DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     mmaakkee prints a message and stops if it cannot open a
     makefile, if a target has no rule and no file by that name
     exists, or if a command run to build a target returns a non-
     zero exit status (unless -ii is in effect).

NNOOTTEESS
     Because macro definitions given on the command line override
     those in the makefile, they are a convenient way to change,
     for example, which compiler or options a makefile uses for a
     single run.
